------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

A cellular gateway (the box in a trailer or a construction site that backhauls
the cameras) gets its provisioning record from the cloud: which APN to dial,
how often to push telemetry, whether LTE or Wi-Fi is the preferred WAN. Every
subsystem on the box — the telemetry uploader, the modem manager, the failover
watchdog — reads that config constantly, from its own thread.

The cloud SDK gives you exactly one entry point:

    struct RawConfig {
        char     apn[32];            // access point name
        uint32_t upload_period_ms;   // telemetry push interval
        uint8_t  wan_priority;       // 0 = LTE first, 1 = Wi-Fi first, ...
        uint32_t version;            // increases on the server
    };

    int cloudcfg_fetch(struct RawConfig *out);

  behaviour                                            returns   blocks
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  config retrieved (often the SAME version as before)     0       0-200 ms
  WAN link down — *out untouched, happens in BURSTS      -1       0-200 ms
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Not thread-safe: it keeps a static session inside. Exactly one thread may
  ever call it, one call at a time.

  Note: in this practice harness the 0-200 ms block is scaled down to 5-50 ms
  so the whole run finishes in about 5 s (SPEC 3.1). The harness also runs one
  phase where the cloud answers instantly and pushes a new version on every
  poll — still legal vendor behaviour, and the only way to make the race in
  Part 1 fire often enough to observe.

A subsystem must never sit in a network call to find out what its APN is, and
must never be handed a half-updated record (a new upload period with the old
APN would mis-provision the modem). So wrap the SDK.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.
  Design and implement an API that lets any number of threads read the current
  configuration. It must be non-blocking (no network wait) and thread-safe.

      int   config_store_init(void);
      void  config_store_deinit(void);

      const struct Config *config_acquire(void);
      void                 config_release(const struct Config *c);
      uint32_t             config_version(void);

  A reader may hold the pointer it got from config_acquire() for as long as it
  likes — across any number of newer publishes — and the object it points at
  must stay valid and unchanged until it calls config_release().

  The interesting part is reclamation. If configs are immutable objects that
  you publish by swapping a pointer, who frees the old one, and when? And in

      p = current;                    /* step A */
      claim_a_reference(p);           /* step B */

  what stops the publisher from freeing *p between A and B? Name the three
  standard answers and say which one belongs on this box, and why.

Part 2.
  Design and implement version history and rollback. A bad config push is the
  classic way to brick a fleet, so the box has to be able to go back.

      const struct Config *config_get_version(uint32_t version);
      int                  config_rollback_to(uint32_t version);
      size_t               config_history(uint32_t *versions_out, size_t max);

  config_history() reports the remembered versions newest first.
  config_get_version() returns NULL for a version that was never seen or has
  already been dropped. config_rollback_to() makes a remembered version
  current again.

  Memory is fixed on this box, so only the last CONFIG_HISTORY_MAX (= 8)
  versions are kept and older ones are evicted. Now answer the hard part:
  what does eviction do about a version a reader is still holding, and what
  happens when every slot in the history is still referenced?

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what the harness marks PASS/FAIL)

  - right after init there is already a config, and its version, fields and
    checksum match what the fake cloud handed out
  - a reader that borrows a config and holds it through 10+ publishes sees the
    exact same bytes the whole time, still checksumming, even after that
    version has been evicted from the history (and is no longer findable)
  - history is exactly 8 entries, newest first, strictly decreasing, and every
    listed version can be looked up and matches the cloud record; lookups of
    evicted and of never-published versions return NULL
  - rollback moves config_version() and config_acquire() to the older version;
    rollback to an evicted or unknown version fails and changes nothing
  - a burst of link-down fetches leaves the last good config in place
  - allocation accounting: configs get freed as they are evicted, live objects
    stay bounded, after deinit exactly the still-borrowed config survives, and
    after the last release allocs == frees (no leaks, no double frees)
  - getter latency: p50 and p99 of a full getter round are microseconds, not
    the milliseconds the vendor call costs
  - 4 readers + 1 publisher for 2 s, then a config storm with concurrent
    rollbacks, with no torn, invented or freed value ever read
  - must also be clean under -fsanitize=thread and -fsanitize=address
    (./main.sh tsan, ./main.sh asan)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Things the interviewer is listening for   (answer these on paper first)

  1. Why can't config_acquire() just call cloudcfg_fetch()? Who calls it, from
     how many threads, and why exactly one?
  2. Immutability: why does "build it fully, then publish one pointer" remove
     the need for a lock on the read side, and what bug does it prevent that a
     field-by-field update under a mutex does not?
  3. Reclamation: the publisher cannot free the old config at swap time. What
     are the owners of one config object, and who ends up calling free()?
  4. The use-after-free window between loading the current pointer and
     claiming a reference on it: describe it, then compare the three fixes —
     a very short mutex, hazard pointers, RCU / epoch reclamation. Which one
     ships on an embedded gateway, and what would make you change your mind?
  5. What exactly may the publisher do while holding your lock? (Hint: not
     fetch, not malloc, not free.) What is the worst time a reader can wait,
     and is "non-blocking" the same thing as "lock-free"?
  6. Part 2: why a bounded ring and not a list, a hash table or a tree, for
     8 entries on this box? What would change your mind at 8000?
  7. Part 2: eviction vs borrowed references. Why is dropping a version from
     the ring NOT a free? What is the peak live memory, and what bounds it?
  8. Part 2: what if the whole ring is still referenced by slow readers — do
     you evict anyway, block the publisher, or fail the publish? Defend it.
  9. Errors: the fetch fails in a burst, or returns the same version as last
     time. What do you publish in each case, and why does publishing the same
     version again cost more than nothing?
 10. Lifetime: a reader is still holding a config when deinit runs. What must
     config_release() still be allowed to do? And how do you stop a thread
     that is parked inside a vendor call for 200 ms?
